<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hsenwi State</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1888 CE (approx.) · capital Hsenwi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful northern Shan state on the Burma-China frontier, at times a major Tai power, later split by the British into North and South Hsenwi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hsenwi, Shan Theinni, emerged as one of the principal Shan states of the northern plateau, with a ruling house that Shan tradition dates to the 13th or 14th century and links to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mong-mao">Mong Mao</a>. It controlled routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy</a> valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hsenwi's saophas ruled a large territory of Shan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-people">Kachin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaung">Palaung</a> and Wa peoples, trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming China</a> called it Muban and recognised its ruler as a pacification commissioner, while Burmese kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> onward claimed overlordship. Succession disputes were common.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1880s Hsenwi was torn by civil war among rival claimants to the saopha title. After the British annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>, the state was divided in 1888 into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/north-hsenwi">North Hsenwi</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/south-hsenwi">South Hsenwi</a>, which survived as separate Shan states until 1959.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hsenwi-state.jpg" alt="Hsenwi State" width="320" height="368"><figcaption>Image: Jolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shan_States-Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1888 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hsenwi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hsenwi, Theinni, Senwi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1594&amp;focus=hsenwi-state" aria-label="Hsenwi State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7292 1331 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hsenwi State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7612 1587L7609 1732M7612 1587L7576 1622M7612 1587L7612 1587M7612 1587L7624 1607"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7609 1732h0M7576 1622h0M7612 1587h0M7624 1607h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7612 1587h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7612 1587h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1594&amp;focus=hsenwi-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

